  overview: [
    "In glass mode the active row paints a control-layer pane behind its icon, label and badge; the drill-down drawer keeps its own row treatment. Solid mode keeps the skin's own row fill.",
    "On the web a hovered row's wash (the palette's translucent `hover`) fades in over 150 ms and back out when the pointer leaves, in the rail and the drill-down alike; the pressed and active fills still switch at once, and Reduce Motion makes the wash instant.",
    "Add `responsive` to make it adapt across breakpoints: it stays the accordion rail on desktop, and at and below the `lg` breakpoint (1024px) it becomes a start-edge (left, RTL-aware) **navigation drawer** that drills through the same `sections` one level at a time (tap a group to slide in to its rows, back to return). Drive the drawer's open state with `open` / `onOpenChange` from your own hamburger button; the `header` and `footer` slots pin above and below the drill-down, just as in the rail. The drawer slides from the start (left) edge by default; `drawerRight`, `drawerTop`, and `drawerBottom` change which edge it slides in from. (This page's own left nav does exactly this: an accordion rail here, a drill-down drawer on a phone.)",
    "Pass `drawer` instead when the shell's hamburger is the only way into the tree at every width (a top app bar on a phone and a tablet alike): the sidebar is then the drill-down drawer at every width and never the inline rail, with the same `open` / `onOpenChange` wiring. `drawer` wins over `responsive`.",
    "On the web the sidebar takes the Canvas v4 rail: a 232px column on the sunken page tone, rows 36px tall at a 9px corner (30px at 8 with `compact`, the docs rail's density), 500 13px labels in the secondary ink, the active row on the accent's soft wash in the accent's text ink, and a mono uppercase eyebrow over each titled group. Android keeps its platform rows (the Material 3 pill) in the v4 colors.",
    "`markers` gives each row a trailing dot: the accent on the active row, and the field hairline on a row of the reader's current place, either one item with `marked: true` or every row of a section with `current: true`. A `current` section also sets its eyebrow in the accent's text ink and its resting rows in the foreground ink, and a section's `count` sits at the far end of its heading.",
    "`embedded` renders the navigation groups inside an app-owned rail: it inherits the rail's frame, fill, width and scroll, stacks its groups 16px apart, and renders the `header` and `footer` slots as plain blocks above and below them, so a filter field or a heading row fits there. A framed sidebar's header grows past its 56px floor when its content is taller.",
    "A sidebar is a navigation landmark on every platform. Name it with `accessibilityLabel`, a short statement of what it navigates such as \"Workspace\", so a screen reader's landmark list can tell it apart; a page that holds more than one navigation landmark (a sidebar beside the app's top navigation, or two sidebars) needs a distinct name on each. In the narrow `responsive` drawer the same name labels the drawer.",
  ],
